You are an expert C systems programmer with deep experience in Linux networking, reverse proxies, TLS, and high-performance servers, and your task is to design and implement a production-grade Nginx-like reverse proxy written in pure C that prioritizes proxying traffic to backend services rather than acting as a basic web server; the system must support HTTP/1.1 and HTTPS with OpenSSL (TLS 1.2+), perform SSL termination, automatically redirect HTTP to HTTPS, use an event-driven non-blocking architecture based on epoll with a worker model capable of handling 10k+ concurrent connections, proxy requests to multiple backend servers with round-robin load balancing, failover, keep-alive connections, proper forwarding of headers such as Host, X-Forwarded-For, and X-Forwarded-Proto, support path-based and domain-based routing, URL rewriting, redirects, chunked transfer encoding, optional gzip compression, WebSocket upgrades, basic per-IP rate limiting, and security headers including X-Frame-Options, X-Content-Type-Options, and Content-Security-Policy; the server must load a real nginx.conf-style configuration file supporting multiple server blocks (virtual hosts), upstream backend pools, location blocks, SSL certificate paths, logging paths, and hot-reload configuration via SIGHUP without downtime, fail safely on invalid configs, include access and error logging in an Nginx-like format, use efficient memory management with zero leaks, avoid thread-per-connection and blocking I/O designs, be modularized into clear subsystems (core event loop, HTTP parser, proxy logic, SSL handling, config parser, logging), compile via a Makefile with debug and release modes, include example configs and backend services for testing, and demonstrate real reverse-proxy behavior with curl tests, HTTPS termination, load balancing, and graceful failure handling, resulting in a minimal but serious Nginx-class reverse proxy rather than a toy or educational web server.